Subject: [PATCH 2/4] Stop a cancelled stream within a frame, not after the
 next chunk

ptt_stream_end() only took effect when the stream thread next called the
chunk callback, i.e. after the next decoded chunk: the decoder waits for
max_chunk_frames (15) latent frames and decodes them in one call, which
with the full-precision Mimi decoder on one thread is ~100-140 ms. A stop
landing mid-sentence took ~120 ms on average.

- PocketTTS::stream() takes an optional cancel flag. The generator thread
  checks it before every frame, the decode loop checks it before waiting
  for a chunk and before the callback, and it is checked between
  sentences.
- PocketTTS::interrupt() sets RunOptions terminate on a RunOptions object
  every session now runs with, so an ONNX Runtime call in flight (a
  15-frame decode, a conditioning pass) returns at once instead of
  finishing. An interrupted call throws; stream() treats that as the
  cancellation, and any other exception still reaches the caller. The
  generator thread now catches exceptions (one used to std::terminate)
  and the decode loop joins it before an exception leaves.
- C API: ptt_stream_cancel(ctx) stops a stream from any thread and returns
  at once; ptt_stream_read() returns 0 once it is cancelled; and
  ptt_stream_end() cancels, so it returns within about a frame too.

Measured through the Dial8 sidecar (2 threads, lsd_steps 8, int8
flow_lm, full-precision decoder, M2 Max), stopping 300 ms after the
first audio: before, ~123 ms; after, under 5 ms (median 0.5 ms over 5
stops), with no CPU used after the stop.
